WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:13.360
The MIT Museum is MIT's window on the world,
and it's also the world's window on MIT.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:20.690
Our job in the Museum is to make research
and innovation as available and accessible

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:26.910
to ordinary folks as we possibly can.

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:31.450
We're trying to test something that Albert
Einstein had called "spooky actions at a distance."

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:36.480
We're testing does the world of the very small,
the world of atoms and parts of atoms, obey

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:38.640
something called quantum entanglement.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:43.400
We're always interested, in the Museum, in
current research at MIT.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:48.500
This particular piece of research intrigued
me because it deals with some of the most

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:53.790
basic ideas in physics, which are in turn
some of the most basic ideas we have about

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:55.760
the way the universe works.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:59.510
We were very, very luck to get support for
the experiment, the series of experiments,

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:01.300
from the U.S. National Science Foundation.

00:01:01.309 --> 00:01:05.619
The NSF always puts a high premium on what
they call "public outreach," or "public engagement."

00:01:05.619 --> 00:01:10.420
There are a lot questions being asked in the
United States today about "what's the point

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:13.310
of science?," and particularly "what's the
point of basic research?"

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:16.670
Research that's asking really fundamental
questions.

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:21.859
And this is a very good example of that, so
this is research which is really probing some

00:01:21.859 --> 00:01:24.729
of the most basic features of the world we
live in.

00:01:24.729 --> 00:01:31.109
Right from the beginning, my colleagues and
I started to brainstorm what we might be able

00:01:31.109 --> 00:01:34.469
to do with this topic, within our museum.

00:01:34.469 --> 00:01:38.549
The project we've conceived is very, if you
like, multimedia.

00:01:38.549 --> 00:01:44.090
We have some exhibition components, we've
got some displays being built in the Museum,

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:49.040
we also have programs, we have some seminars
and some educational programs.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:52.720
But alongside that, maybe some drama might
also help here.

00:01:52.720 --> 00:02:00.200
We have a unique, and created just for this
project, one-act play that was developed by

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:04.400
the Central Square Theatre as part of the
Catalyst Collaborative at MIT.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:09.540
I had never even heard of quantum entanglement
before, and it was just mind-boggling.

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:15.480
And, of course, the idea ... now you're going
to write a 20-minute play about this thing

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:19.711
that's really hard to visualize, we can't
really draw a picture of it ... that was a

00:02:19.711 --> 00:02:21.800
really big challenge, and I like challenges.

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:23.390
I like challenges a lot.

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:29.660
The play will run in the Museum on many occasions,
so Museum visitors can come and experience it.

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:36.560
And before or after, of course, explore the
exhibit with the student-made components.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:43.201
So this exhibition is a pretty concise installation
of the concept, where we have MIT students

00:02:43.201 --> 00:02:48.510
who are physicists and mathematicians coming
in to actually think about the subject matter

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:49.950
and how to represent it visually.

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:55.600
Here in the Museum, we love to work with MIT
students--undergraduates and grad students.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:59.450
We think that they get a lot out of learning
how to communicate about science, and we get

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:04.710
a lot from their energy, their enthusiasm,
and their smarts, really.

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:11.280
Our project is called Spooky Action, and it
is a way for people to experience quantum

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:17.290
entanglement, specifically a process whereby
two particles can actually influence each other's

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:19.240
behavior just by being physically separated.

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:26.160
And so we tried to visualize that effect by
using a strobe light to highlight certain

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:27.800
positions of the rotating ball.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:32.861
And so, the ball's rotating at a constant
rate, it's always continuously moving, but

00:03:32.861 --> 00:03:36.390
the strobe effect makes it look like it's
frozen in space.

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:41.850
There are multiple ways that museum visitors
can explore this concept: through the performance,

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:46.710
through the exhibit, and through the very
many public programs that we're running in

00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:49.450
conjunction with the entire topic itself.

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:55.069
I think we enjoy the challenge of coming up
with new ways of connecting with audiences.

00:03:55.069 --> 00:04:02.220
We try to create an engaging setting, maybe
a non-traditional setting, where folks might feel welcome.

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:09.440
If we can excite even a couple of adults or
children to want to take that deeper dive,

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:13.959
that really was the overarching goal here,
that gave us the freedom to try these other

00:04:13.959 --> 00:04:18.180
modalities and mediums in how we communicated
this topic.

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:20.959
The MIT Museum wants to be an experimenter.

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:25.090
It wants to find new ways of taking science
to new audiences.

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:29.980
And I think this is going to take a step further
forward ... To really say, lets wrestle with these

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.289
hard concepts and not shy away from them.

00:04:32.289 --> 00:04:36.800
What can we do to try to help people get their
hands around this kind of complicated thing?

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:42.370
I think you wrap all of those together, you
have a really amazing experience for an audience.

00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:45.120
I think they'll say, wow, this is wondrous.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:48.770
And if we can inspire a sense of wonder, I
think that's really exciting.

